<item>
  <title><![CDATA[How the ferry schedule shows up in house prices]]></title>
  <link>https://example.com/notes/ferry-schedule-and-house-prices/</link>
  <pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
  <guid isPermaLink="false">https://example.com/notes/ferry-schedule-and-house-prices/</guid>
  <description/>
  <content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:html -->
<img class="prose-img" src="/wp-content/themes/shoal/assets/img/blog-ferry.webp" alt="A ferry crossing the sound at dusk" width="1920" height="1072" loading="eager" decoding="async" data-astro-cid-h3xj43oc><div class="prose-body" data-astro-cid-h3xj43oc><p>On the islands, distance is measured in sailings, not miles. A buyer who commutes to the mainland three days a week will pay for a house that makes the 5:40 boat without a car.</p><p>Look at the sold prices of the last two years and you will see it: homes within a ten-minute walk of the Teal Harbor dock sell for more per square foot than larger homes on Brannock&#39;s north shore.</p><p>If you do not commute, that premium is money you can keep. If you do, check the winter schedule, not the summer one. It has fewer boats.</p><button type="button" class="btn btn-solid" data-open-contact>Ask Dana</button></div>
<!-- /wp:html -->]]></content:encoded>
  <excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[Why a house ten minutes from the first boat can cost more than a bigger one with a better view.]]></excerpt:encoded>
